From: Jordan Sparks <sparksj@ucs.orst.edu>
Date: Fri, 2 Dec 1994 17:32:01 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-12-87] singularity

On Fri, 2 Dec 1994, Godshatter wrote:

> >From pmetzger@lehman.com Sat Apr 24 02:50:40 1993
> Date: Wed, 14 Apr 1993 18:02:11 -0400
> From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
> 
> ...Consider: once we can replace our neurons with nanomachines,
> we will be able to speed up our thought processes by at least 100
> Million times. (I personally feel the possibility of accelerating it
> by hundreds of billions of times is well within the realm of
> possibility, but lets be conservative for the moment.) The individual
> who does this will suddenly find that for every day that passes to
> other people, 27 THOUSAND years have passed in subjective time to him.
> Consider what that means: in the time it takes you to say "hello",
> several YEARS will have passed to that individual. Once we launch our
> first few people over the edge with that sort of speedup, by the next
> afternoon more will have happened than has happened in five times our
> recorded history...

This is a bunch of garbage.  First, he's assuming computers will be 100 
million times more efficient than the human brain when the first upload 
occurs.  Why would we wait so long?  Surely we would do it much sooner.  
You also have to create a VR which puts huge limits on speed.  The first 
uploads will probably be _slower_ than biologicals.  The advantage will 
lie in flexibility in thought and not having to hassle with sleep, etc.  
Furthermore, there is no 'edge' to launch them over.  There is no reason 
why it will not be a steadily increasing slope.

They would not be able to redesign the solar system in a day, either.  
There are natural limits to energy dissipation, working against gravity, 
acceleration, etc which would make it take much longer.

It makes nice science fiction, though.


