Subject: [#94-12-89] Trash privatizing

	A thread just started on my San Diego list about county
	trash privatizing, and I thought it might be an interesting	
	enough topic for libertarians everywhere.

What's the Libertarian position on the county trash privatizing?
(I know, all for privatizing...but the county seems to be going
about it with lots of strings and hooks...?)

        I'm also interested in the answer, so please make sure to
        cc sdlp@lumina.ucsd.edu when you reply :-)

        I'd like to throw in curbside recycling as well.  I may not
        understand the situation properly, but I know some parts of
        the city supply bins to the citizens and I gather that it is
        currently illegal for private parties to collect those
        recyclables, right?  And for private parties to collect trash
        from curbside trash cans too, right?  If so, then it would
        seem to me that a straightforward first step would be to
        legalize competition for curbside pickups.  Heck, recycling
        might even become a profitable business :-)

        The other side of the problem, where to put all that trash,
        seems a little trickier (but that's probably because I don't
        know much about the problem).  I suppose this issue dovetails
        with that of hazardous waste disposal and that of "pollution"
        in general.  I know that some "trash" is burnable, so it can
        possibly serve as a low-grade source of energy.  Some "trash",
        namely organic byproducts, can be "harvested" through micro-
        biology to produce methane gas, another source of fuel.

        These techniques all seem to rely heavily on separating "trash"
        into re-usable types:  recyclable materials (glass, aluminum),
        burnable materials (paper), methane-producing materials (organics),
        etc.  So, I would think that some market-incentives would be
        invented and seized upon by self-interested individuals.  For
        instance, under a market-organized environment, people might
        actually be _paid_ for separating out their "trash" into usable
        types, and the quantity of trash to be carted away that _isn't_
        usable would be reduced ... suggesting a market-incentive for
        pricing curbside trash-pickup services based on the weight (and
        or volume) of the trash to be hauled away.  Of course, some
        people will benefit by having trash picked up more or less 
        frequently than the city-govt-standard of once-a-week.

Re:
the county seems to be going about it with lots of strings and hooks

        This may be endemic to government; for some reason they always
        seem to want to get their tentacles wrapped around our activities.
        Maybe they just think it's their job to "govern" or "control",
        so whenever something comes up for consideration, their first
        thought is "how can we govern this?" ... if all you have is a
        hammer, everything starts looking like a nail.  As an example of
        this effect, I offer "free trade".  We all know that "free trade"
        doesn't require 22,000+ pages of a GATT treaty, or however many
        thousand pages of a NAFTA treaty, but apparently the politicians
        and bureaucrats think otherwise.

