Subject: [#94-12-90] Basics: Coercion

At 9:48 PM 12/1/94, Fred Hapgood wrote:
>My point is not that this is a good or bad thing, but that it is
>absurd to associate private courts with the elimination of
>coercion.

Sounds like a semantic point. Are you trying to point out that it requires
force to enforce a contract?

Or are you trying to get to the deeper point that it's probably impossible
to live in a contractarian utopia where everyone has agreed in some
substantial fashion to all the rules, obligations and requirements they
have to live up to?

The question is, will there necessarily be times when a court has to
enforce some sort of judgement against persons who can truly claim that
they never agreed to any contract that bound them to respect that
judgement? I think so. This is the problem of the "outsider". While a
judgement agency may have motives to treat outsiders reasonably, they
cannot constrain themselves to never enforce anything against outsiders, or
they become pretty useless. So social life requires coercion.

On the other hand, to lump all kinds of "coercion" together is also pretty
useless. A semantics which categorizes eviction of trespassers together
with the holocaust is of no use to anyone.

"other" Dave
tburns@mason1.gmu.edu



