Subject: [#94-12-91] The Machinery of Friedman

Fred Hapgood asks..

>...Let's say I want to live in a society that prohibits child abuse
>and am willing to pay for my preferences.  How do I do that under
>Friedman's scheme?  

Tim Starr says...

   I'd imagine that the first thing you'd do is move into your
   own proprietary community in which "child abuse" defined
   the way you like would be prohibited.

What??  Move?  Why would I want to move?  Why wouldn't I just pay
guys to go around and take kids away from parents who are giving
them the wrong kind of attention?  There are lots of people
on the North American continent with intense feelings about this
subject.  If we pooled our assets we could cobble together one
helluva police force.  Why wouldn't we just do that?  What does
moving have to do with anything?

Tim Starr also says ..

   David's economic analysis is first-rate (except for his
   lack of appreciation for the knowledge problem).  His use
   of philosophical concepts is a bit sloppy when it comes to
   "government."  To be more precise, what anarchists object
   to is the State, defined as a territorial monopoly on
   legitimate violence.

OK.  Suppose I did for some reason move into one of these
'proprietary communities' you are talking about.  Wouldn't the
agency responsible for law in that community have a territorial
monopoly on legitimate violence?  And wouldn't that make it a
State in the sense you are defining here?


