Date: Fri, 2 Dec 1994 21:37:56 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-12-95] Basics: Coercion

Fred Hapgood:
> >My point is not that this is a good or bad thing, but that it is
> >absurd to associate private courts with the elimination of
> >coercion.

T. David Burns: 
> Sounds like a semantic point. Are you trying to point out that it requires
> force to enforce a contract?

I presume that enforcement of a contract is not considered initiation
of force,  or else is the only exception to that principle.  
This includes enforcement of contracts against third party attacks,
ie property rights.  Under these conditions it seems to me that
there has to be either non-violent means of protecting property,
or inter-jurisdictional agreeement on what constitutes property
rights enforceable against third parties, or a balance of power
between the conflicting jurisdictions (the former two being
the most desirable states).

